{"archetypes":[{"name":"fd3-capture","focus_area":"correctness","weight":1,"rationale":"The plan directs ci-failed-jobs.sh to emit FAILED_JOBS_COUNT via emit_kv on FD-3 and separately says the caller will 'parse its FD-3 FAILED_JOBS_COUNT', but whether FD-3 writes are capturable by the parent depends on whether the invocation is wrapped in command substitution — the plan never specifies this, and the gh-run-logs.sh call nearby uses direct invocation (no command substitution), which would make FD-3 writes go to the parent's quiet log rather than any capture buffer.","prompt_body":"Examine how lib-quiet.sh's emit_kv routes output when LARCH_QUIET_PID equals the subprocess PID vs the parent PID, and specifically whether a subprocess that calls larch_quiet_init writes its FD-3 output to the caller's capture buffer or to the inherited quiet-log FD. Compare with the existing ci-rerun-failed.sh and gh-run-logs.sh invocation patterns in run_evaluate_failure to determine which style (command substitution capturing stdout vs direct call) is consistent with 'parse FAILED_JOBS_COUNT from FD-3'. Identify any invocation pattern mismatch that would silently produce an empty or stale FAILED_JOBS_COUNT and cause the per-job loop to be skipped when it should run. Cite specific file paths and line ranges for any issues found, and follow the output-format rules from your outer wrapper exactly."},{"name":"eval-injection","focus_area":"security","weight":1,"rationale":"The plan's run_captured_cmd_then_fix_loop uses eval \"$LOCAL_CMD\" where LOCAL_CMD flows from ci-failed-jobs.sh's case-statement mapping over job names sourced from gh run view --json, and the matrix-shard normalization extracts a shard index from a parenthesized suffix — a crafted job name could bypass the case catchall or inject into the shard variable before it reaches eval.","prompt_body":"Trace the full data path from the raw job name string returned by gh run view --json jobs through the matrix-shard normalization (stripping the parenthesized suffix to extract a bare shard index) and the case-statement dispatch to the LOCAL_CMD value that reaches eval in run_captured_cmd_then_fix_loop. Verify that the case catchall '*) no-local-equivalent' fully prevents any non-matching job-name string from ever populating LOCAL_CMD, and check whether the shard-index extraction (e.g. cutting the digit from 'test-harnesses (7)') is done with a pattern that also sanitizes inputs like 'test-harnesses (7; rm -rf /)' or 'test-harnesses ()'. Also check if the --output-tsv TSV format uses a delimiter that a job name containing a literal tab could corrupt, allowing a row to be parsed as two columns with a different LOCAL_CMD. Cite specific file paths and line ranges for any issues found, and follow the output-format rules from your outer wrapper exactly."},{"name":"make-probe-reliability","focus_area":"correctness","weight":1,"rationale":"The plan detects whether a sharded Makefile target like 'make test-harnesses-7' exists via 'make -n test-harnesses-7 2>/dev/null' and falls back to 'make test-harnesses' on non-zero exit, but GNU make's exit code for a non-existent target is version-dependent and 'make -n' may succeed (exit 0) on implicit rules, silently choosing the sharded command over the safe fallback and running an incorrect target.","prompt_body":"Check the Makefile for the test-harnesses target definition to determine whether sharded targets like test-harnesses-7 exist today and whether the Makefile uses pattern rules or explicit rules that would affect 'make -n' exit-code behavior for non-existent sharded targets. Verify what exit code 'make -n <nonexistent-target>' produces on the macOS Bash 3.2 environment this repo targets (where GNU make may behave differently from Linux CI), and assess whether a false-positive detection would cause the per-job loop to invoke a nonexistent or no-op sharded target instead of the safe 'make test-harnesses' fallback. Also assess whether running 'make test-harnesses' (the full matrix) as a fallback for any single-shard failure is an acceptable cost at the outer _max_fix=3 cap or whether it could cause a resource-exhaustion scenario in practice. Cite specific file paths and line ranges for any issues found, and follow the output-format rules from your outer wrapper exactly."}]}
